        "math_academy_id": "q-49242",
        "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$, calculate\n${\\int }_{4}^{0}(2x-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factoring out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{0}(2x-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{4}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x-6{\\int }_{4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}|}_{4}^{0}-6(-2) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}-{4}^{2})+12 \\\\  & =-16+12 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
